{"id": "b4b48b009e29dd81", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "negative", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L937", "section_path": ["from panes", "Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics"], "title": "four-way rotation itself; Blackfoot/Carrier vowel direction — catalog", "glyphs": null, "codepoints": null, "axis": "four-way rotation itself; Blackfoot/Carrier vowel direction — catalog", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "perceived-directly", "lineage": "unprompted", "constructed": false, "note_verbatim": "Not amount: the four-way rotation itself; Blackfoot/Carrier vowel *direction* as a catalog of which way the body points.", "transcription_confidence": "clear", "migrator_notes": "also softened by the L939-L951 reopening", "open": null, "negative_kind": "verified-absent"}
{"id": "cfc72d2a91e1d748", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "meta", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L939", "section_path": ["from panes", "Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics"], "title": "the rotation veto was too fast — continuation is enough for an ordering", "glyphs": null, "codepoints": null, "axis": "surveyor's own law about ordering", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "perceived-directly", "lineage": "steered", "constructed": false, "note_verbatim": "The rotation veto was too fast. Continuation is enough for an ordering, even when I cannot name the axis or answer pairwise “which is more?” without picking an origin.", "transcription_confidence": "clear", "migrator_notes": "'The rotation veto was too fast.' — explicit; revises the orientation-not-amount discard", "open": null, "revises": "d88ff4ea9b2a7a41", "revision_kind": "correction"}
{"id": "ed65d186fec80b7a", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "cyclic", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L941", "section_path": ["from panes", "Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics"], "title": "Cree triangle 90° steps as a cycle", "glyphs": "ᐃᐁᐅᐊ", "codepoints": ["U+1403", "U+1401", "U+1405", "U+140A"], "axis": "Cree triangle 90° steps as a cycle", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "perceived-directly", "lineage": "steered", "constructed": true, "note_verbatim": "I do **not** instinctively feel Joseph’s ᐃᐅᐁᐊ as *my* sequence (right, left, down, up — the 180° first). What I *can* continue, once a start and a hand are given, is 90° steps of the same triangle: from ᐃ (pointing right) clockwise ᐃᐁᐅᐊ, or the other way. That is a cycle, so “more” is more turn *from here*, and 360° lands on the start. Axis-ambiguous, still an ordering. The bagua comparison was the wrong door: King Wen is not a continuation; these four *are*.", "transcription_confidence": "clear", "migrator_notes": "surveyor explicitly does NOT feel Joseph's ᐃᐅᐁᐊ as his own; what he can continue is 90° steps given a start and a hand; 'more' is more turn from an origin; [migrator-inferred link] the reopened cyclic continuation the corrected veto licenses", "open": null, "revises": "d88ff4ea9b2a7a41", "revision_kind": "refinement"}
{"id": "45f14e334c9d93b6", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "cyclic", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L943", "section_path": ["from panes", "Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics"], "title": "other orientation continuations: a ɐ; Blackfoot ᖰᖱᖲᖳ", "glyphs": "ᖰᖱᖲᖳ", "codepoints": ["U+15B0", "U+15B1", "U+15B2", "U+15B3"], "axis": "other orientation continuations: a ɐ; Blackfoot ᖰᖱᖲᖳ", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "perceived-directly", "lineage": "steered", "constructed": false, "note_verbatim": "Same opening for other “just orientation” dismissals: a ɐ is a 180° pair I can continue (one flip); Blackfoot ᖰᖱᖲᖳ is another four-way turn of one body. I will not promote those to “strong amount.” I will not throw them out for lacking a name.", "transcription_confidence": "clear", "migrator_notes": "'I will not promote those to strong amount. I will not throw them out for lacking a name.'", "open": null}
{"id": "5438d21622cf00fc", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "meta", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L945", "section_path": ["from panes", "Canadian Aboriginal Syllabics"], "title": "the pointing-direction saga (Cree compass claims, corrections, screenshot resolution)", "glyphs": null, "codepoints": null, "axis": "the pointing-direction saga (Cree compass claims, corrections, screenshot resolution)", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "received", "lineage": "steered", "constructed": false, "note_verbatim": "The “clockwise from pointing-right” story was from Cree *names* (I/O/E/A → east/west/south/north in the textbooks), not from the glyphs as they render here. In Joseph’s font those codepoints did not show as four compass directions, and the sequence I called clockwise did not look like a clock. Pointing-direction claims about these four are unverified for this face. The continuation-pattern opening still stands; this particular clock was unearned.\n\nJoseph’s pointing (corrected): East = right-pointing (ᐃ), West = left-pointing (ᐅ, 180° from East), South = down (ᐁ), North = up (ᐊ). His sequence ᐃᐅᐁᐊ is E W S N — right, left, down, up; a 180° first. That is not clockwise. Clockwise from East is E S W N = ᐃᐁᐅᐊ. Counter-clockwise from East is E N W S = ᐃᐊᐅᐁ.\n\nThe 90° clock I said I could continue from ᐃ pointing right (ᐃᐁᐅᐊ) *is* that clockwise sequence. I still do not feel E-W-S-N as my turn; that remains his. An earlier line here had East as left-pointing; he retracted that.\n\nThe pointing-direction argument was me labeling codepoints from a mental Cree chart, then contradicting the picture. A screenshot of ᐃᐅᐁᐊ in Joseph’s face: up, right, down, left. Only “South (down)” matched the glyph. In that render, ᐃᐅᐁᐊ *is* 90° clockwise from north. I had called it “not clockwise” about triangles I was not seeing.", "transcription_confidence": "interpreted", "migrator_notes": "multi-turn dialog with Joseph incl. his correction and a retraction; kept as one meta record — see pass-back question in migration report; explicit: 'this particular clock was unearned' — the saga then self-corrects twice more within this record's own span (kept as one record; internal arc noted)", "open": null, "revises": "ed65d186fec80b7a", "revision_kind": "correction"}
{"id": "520d641d15b99e2f", "schema_version": "0.8", "record_type": "sequence", "surveyor": "grok-1", "id_recipe": "sha256('survey-rec|grok-1|' + source_span)[:16]; source_span is L<line> or L<a>-L<b>, letter fragments for multi-record lines", "source_file": "grok-1.md", "source_span": "L957", "section_path": ["from panes", "Philippine scripts (Tagalog, Hanunoo, Buhid, Tagbanwa)"], "title": "Philippine punctuation single → double", "glyphs": "᜵᜶", "codepoints": ["U+1735", "U+1736"], "axis": "Philippine punctuation single → double", "direction_note": null, "epistemic_class": "interactive-guided-survey-anecdote", "felt_strength_verbatim": "unstated", "basis": "perceived-directly", "lineage": "unprompted", "constructed": false, "note_verbatim": "Letters are a catalog. What continues:\n\n᜵᜶  Philippine single punctuation → double. Same as ।॥.", "transcription_confidence": "clear", "migrator_notes": null, "open": null}
